  "cause": "**On Monday Washington told Congress it intends to take two countries off a trade list, and gave the reason in a letter.** **Trump** notified Congress of the removal of **South Africa and Rwanda** from the **African Growth and Opportunity Act** — the programme that lets eligible African states export thousands of products to the US duty-free — **effective 1 January 2027**. US Trade Representative **Jamieson Greer** said an annual review found both countries engaging in practices \"inconsistent with the programme's eligibility requirements\", citing **barriers to US trade and investment** and **national security and foreign policy interests**; for South Africa the US pointed to **corruption**. In his letter to **President Cyril Ramaphosa**, Trump wrote that Pretoria's actions on international security and its relations with \"governments of concern to Washington\" risked undermining US national security interests, and accused it of significant barriers including **agricultural market access, regulatory requirements, investment protections and intellectual property**. **The numbers on both sides are public, and they point in opposite directions.** South Africa's Trade Minister **Parks Tau** called the decision \"unfortunate\", said Washington was **raising new issues that had not featured in negotiations begun in 2025**, and promised to \"accelerate the diversification of South Africa's export markets to protect jobs\". He also put the exposure in perspective: about **89%** of South African exports to the US entered under ordinary **WTO most-favoured-nation** terms in 2025, around **10%** benefited from AGOA — down from **28%** in 2024 — and AGOA-covered exports fell to about **$1.7bn** from **$4bn** the previous year. Bilateral trade reached **$28.8bn** in 2025; more than **600 US companies** operate in South Africa supporting around **134,600 jobs**; South Africa supplies **12 of the 50 minerals** the US lists as critical and **70%** of American isotopes used, among other things, in **cancer treatment**. US Ambassador **Leo Brent Bozell III** called it \"a predictable and preventable outcome resulting from the rejection by South Africa's government of real concerns raised by the US\". **In Rwanda's case the programme is withdrawn while the army is under sanctions.** Washington cited **human rights, political pluralism, the rule of law and workers' rights** — and the **US Treasury** said Rwanda's **defence ministry** directed thousands of troops to fight alongside **M23** rebels in eastern **DR Congo**, providing weapons, equipment, logistics and training. Rwanda denies backing M23 \"despite overwhelming evidence\". Treasury had sanctioned that ministry **barely a week earlier**. Rwanda's exports to the US rose to **$43.3m** in 2025 from **$13.1m** in 2024. Both decisions sit inside a wider rupture: Trump has accused South Africa of persecuting its white minority, launched an asylum programme for white Afrikaners, and repeated widely discredited claims of genocide.",
  "bullets": [
   "**A trade preference is being used as a conduct lever whose justification is not trade** — \"barriers to investment\" and \"corruption\" listed beside \"relations with governments of concern\" and an accusation of racial persecution; the direct effect is that AGOA's eligibility test has become a loyalty test; the compound consequence is that every other beneficiary now knows the review is discretionary, which makes the whole programme a rolling incentive to anticipate Washington rather than a rule to plan against — and plans are what factories are built on",
   "**The punishment is smaller than the relationship, which is why it will not produce the concession** — 10% of exports under AGOA against $28.8bn of bilateral trade, 600 US companies and 134,600 jobs, 12 of 50 critical minerals and 70% of medical isotopes; the direct effect is that South Africa loses a shrinking margin rather than a market; the compound consequence is that a sanction which cannot hurt enough to compel becomes a signal that cannot be withdrawn without looking weak — a sanction that must escalate to work, and must fail if it does not",
   "**Pretoria's answer is diversification, which is the outcome Washington says it wants to avoid** — \"accelerate the diversification of export markets to protect jobs\", said by the trade minister within hours; the direct effect is that years spent building AGOA-dependent capacity get redirected rather than recovered; the compound consequence is that the countries with most to lose from US withdrawal have the strongest incentive to build alternatives, so each removal hands the competition a client it did not have to win",
   "**Removing Rwanda ten days after sanctioning its defence ministry is a sequence, not two events** — Treasury sanctions, then AGOA removal, with M23 spelled out in both; the direct effect is that the trade measure reads as an enforcement annex to the sanctions; the compound consequence is that the pressure lands on an export sector employing civilians while the named actor — a defence ministry directing troops — is the one institution a trade instrument cannot touch, so the cost is transferred downward and the behaviour is untouched",
   "**A programme that ends by executive notification ends the certainty it was created to provide** — Congress notified, 1 January 2027 effective, annual reviews with unwritten criteria; the direct effect is that every beneficiary must now price political risk into a duty-free margin; the compound consequence is that the US loses the one thing AGOA was buying — predictability — and gains a lever that works once per country, because after the first use nobody builds a supply chain on a preference a letter can end",
   "**The hinge is whether eligibility is written as criteria or applied as judgement, and whether the review is answered in public** — the moment the \"new issues\" Tau describes are tabled in the negotiations that began in 2025 rather than announced after them; the moment the annual review publishes findings a beneficiary can answer before removal takes effect; the moment the January 2027 date is used to fix the specific barriers named — agricultural access, intellectual property, investment protection — instead of as a deadline for a political concession; and the moment the minerals and isotopes inside the relationship are counted as leverage on both sides rather than only one. §28:374 describes what happens when a jurisdiction mistakes its own fairness for the creational standard: the decisions and punishments that proceed from it \"directly counteract the creational laws.\""
  ],
  "verse": "What is sad about your behaviour of the personal and social fairness and the thereout resulting laws as well as your jurisdictions and many decisions and punishments resulting thereout, is that they directly counteract the creational laws.",
  "verseSrc": "Chapter 28, Verse 374",
  "hinge": "The cause is not the tariff line and it is not AGOA. It is the choice — by Washington, whether eligibility is a published standard or a discretionary judgement; by Pretoria, whether diversification is a threat or a plan with a date; by the companies employing 134,600 people, whether they speak before 1 January 2027 or after; and by the other beneficiaries, whether they treat this review as an exception or as the template for their own. §28:374 does not say that human jurisdiction is unnecessary; it says a punishment that counteracts the creational law damages the one who imposes it as surely as the one who receives it."
